<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="A 1978 Olympia racing bicycle with Campagnolo Nuovo Gran Sport, Universal brakes, an Ofmega chainset and Campagnolo hubs."/><meta property="og:type" content="article"/><meta property="og:title" content="1978 Olympia racing bicycle · The Evolution of Bikes"/><meta property="og:description" content="An orange Italian steel racer, formerly in the collection, documented through its owner’s photographs and component notes."/><meta property="og:image" content={heroSocial}/><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"/><title>1978 Olympia racing bicycle · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="bikes" danishPath="/cykler/olympia-1978/"/><main class="atala-page">
<header class="quick-hero"><p class="section-label">Previously in the collection · Italy · stated 1978</p><h1>Olympia<br/><em>1978</em></h1><p>An orange Italian steel racer with down-tube friction shifting and a mixed build of Campagnolo, Universal and Ofmega parts.</p></header>
<figure class="quick-pass"><OptimizedImage src={heroImage} alt="Orange 1978 Olympia racing bicycle seen from the drive side" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high"/><figcaption>The Olympia from the drive side. Year and component details were supplied by its former owner. The bicycle is no longer in the collection. Photo: The Evolution of Bikes.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="atala-facts" aria-label="Key facts"><div><strong>1978</strong><span>Stated year</span></div><div><strong>Steel</strong><span>Lugged frame</span></div><div><strong>Campagnolo</strong><span>Friction shifting</span></div><div><strong>Formerly</strong><span>In the collection</span></div></section>
<section class="atala-story"><div><p class="section-label">What we know</p><h2>From a small bicycle shop in Verona</h2></div><div><p>I bought the bicycle from a small dealer in Verona during one of my trips to Italy in search of vintage bicycles. It subsequently came home and became part of the collection.</p><p>The bicycle is recorded as a 1978 Olympia. The photographs show the Olympia name on the down tube, its head badge and a traditional lugged steel frame.</p><p>Its front and rear derailleurs and down-tube levers are recorded as Campagnolo Nuovo Gran Sport. Universal supplied the brakes and levers, while Ofmega supplied the chainset.</p></div></section>
<section class="atala-details"><figure><OptimizedImage src={shifters} alt="Campagnolo friction levers on the orange Olympia down tube"/><figcaption>Campagnolo friction levers required the rider to find the chain’s quiet position.</figcaption></figure><figure><OptimizedImage src={rearDerailleur} alt="Campagnolo rear derailleur and freewheel on the Olympia"/><figcaption>The rear derailleur is recorded as Nuovo Gran Sport.</figcaption></figure><figure><OptimizedImage src={headbadge} alt="Olympia head badge and Universal front brake"/><figcaption>The Olympia badge sits above a Universal front brake.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="atala-components"><div><p class="section-label">A mixed Italian build</p><h2>One bicycle, several makers</h2></div><div><p>Campagnolo supplied the gear parts and, according to the owner’s notes, Super Record hubs. Universal brakes and an Ofmega chainset came from other Italian makers; the handlebar is recorded as 3TTT.</p><p>The wheels are recorded with Ambrosio tubular rims. The Selle Italia Grand Prix Professional saddle is dated in the notes to the late 1960s or early 1970s. It therefore predates the stated frame year and should be read as part of the bicycle’s later configuration rather than confirmed factory equipment.</p><p>The photographs document the build as it survived. They do not establish an original 1978 factory specification.</p></div></section>
<section class="atala-detail-pair"><figure><OptimizedImage src={cockpit} alt="Handlebar, brake levers and front end of the Olympia"/><figcaption>Universal levers, red tape and external cables keep the mechanics visible.</figcaption></figure><div><p class="section-label">Recorded as found</p><h2>Age was not polished away</h2><p>Small paint chips, patinated metal and aged tyres remain visible. The photographs record the bicycle before it left the collection rather than presenting it as new.</p><p>That sets a useful limit: the mounted parts can be described, but without a catalogue they cannot be claimed as Olympia’s original factory specification.</p></div></section>
<section class="atala-details"><figure><OptimizedImage src={rearBrake} alt="Universal rear brake and Ofmega chainset on the Olympia"/><figcaption>Universal brake and Ofmega chainset in the same practical build.</figcaption></figure><figure><OptimizedImage src={stemAndHeadtube} alt="Stem, handlebar and head tube of the Olympia seen from above"/><figcaption>The stem remains unidentified. The photograph preserves its visible form without assigning a maker.</figcaption></figure><figure><OptimizedImage src={rearDerailleur} alt="Close view of the Olympia drivetrain"/><figcaption>The drivetrain records a bicycle that was ridden and maintained.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="koga-specs"><div><p class="section-label">Previously in the collection</p><h2>Brief specification</h2></div><dl><div><dt>Maker</dt><dd>Olympia</dd></div><div><dt>Year</dt><dd>1978, stated</dd></div><div><dt>Frame</dt><dd>Lugged steel</dd></div><div><dt>Gears</dt><dd>Campagnolo Nuovo Gran Sport, stated</dd></div><div><dt>Brakes and levers</dt><dd>Universal</dd></div><div><dt>Chainset</dt><dd>Ofmega</dd></div><div><dt>Handlebar</dt><dd>3TTT, stated</dd></div><div><dt>Hubs</dt><dd>Campagnolo Super Record, stated</dd></div><div><dt>Rims</dt><dd>Ambrosio tubular, stated</dd></div><div><dt>Saddle</dt><dd>Selle Italia Grand Prix Professional, stated late 1960s to early 1970s</dd></div><div><dt>Status</dt><dd>Previously in the collection</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="atala-story period-sources"><div><p class="section-label">Basis</p><h2>Photographs and owner’s notes</h2></div><div><p>This page is based on seven photographs and the component notes kept with the bicycle in the collection archive.</p><p>The stated year and model names that cannot be read directly are presented as owner-supplied information. No contemporary Olympia catalogue has been used to establish an original factory build.</p></div></section>
<section class="atala-closing"><p>The Olympia is no longer in the collection. Its photographs preserve a specific 1970s racer with an Italian mixture of parts—and leave the unknown fields open.</p><div class="bike-bottom-nav"><a href={`${base}/en/bikes/`}>← Back to bicycles</a><a href={`${base}/en/periods/1970-1979/`}>View the period 1970–1979 →</a></div></section>
</main><EnglishFooter/></body></html>
